        "summary":  "AWS released Strands Box, an open-source sandbox tool designed to restrict AI agent actions through operating system-level isolation and policy enforcement using the Dogwood policy language. Currently available in developer preview for macOS with Apple silicon, the tool allows developers to set behavioral limits on autonomous agents while reducing reliance on agent frameworks\u0027 built-in permission mechanisms. The tool represents AWS\u0027s effort to provide infrastructure-level guardrails independent of the agent framework being used.",
        "execSummary":  "AWS Strands Box signals that hyperscaler-grade agent containment is moving from proprietary agent-framework features to OS-level infrastructure, a maturation milestone for enterprise-grade agent deployments. For enterprise architects, the value proposition is portability: Dogwood policies that define what an agent can do are framework-agnostic, reducing lock-in risk as the agent platform landscape consolidates. Customer-support operations leaders should note that this class of tooling enables blast-radius controls, limiting the damage a misbehaving agent can cause, which is a prerequisite for giving agents broader autonomy over sensitive customer systems. The caveat is the current macOS/Apple silicon constraint, which limits immediate enterprise applicability; watch for a Linux/cloud-native release before including Strands Box in production architecture planning.",
        "vendorLean":  "AWS — promotes Strands agent framework and AWS infrastructure for agent deployment",
