        "title":  "Build self-improving document processing using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore",
        "source":  "AWS Machine Learning Blog",
        "url":  "https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/build-self-improving-document-processing-using-amazon-bedrock-agentcore/",
        "date":  "2026-10-14",
        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "This technical guide demonstrates how to build an intelligent document processing system using Amazon Bedrock AgentCore, deploying eight specialized AI agents across two graph-based workflows to classify, extract, validate, and learn from business documents autonomously. The system reduces manual data entry errors and adapts to new document formats over time through self-training. Estimated monthly cost for 1,000 processed documents runs approximately $58, with processing time dropping from 3 minutes 49 seconds on first run to 1 minute 30 seconds on subsequent similar documents.",
        "execSummary":  "Agentic document processing directly addresses one of the highest-friction, highest-error operational areas in enterprise back offices, with AWS quantifying the cost at nearly $20,000 per information worker annually. The self-improving architecture is the significant differentiator here: rather than requiring ongoing ML engineering to retrain models on new document types, the system learns from corrections autonomously, lowering the total cost of ownership for high-document-volume operations like claims processing, AP/AR, and contract management. The $58/month cost estimate for 1,000 documents is compelling but leaders should note that 85% is model inference, which scales linearly and can spike unpredictably on complex document batches. Organizations evaluating this should prioritize the governance layer: who reviews agent corrections, and how do errors get caught before they compound across training cycles.",
