        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Researchers from Harvard Business School, MIT, and the University of Washington found that AI systems providing written explanations for their recommendations caused human evaluators to become less likely to independently verify those decisions. The study showed that narrative explanations degraded decision quality in early-stage innovation screening, leading people to reject promising ideas and accept inferior ones. Counterintuitively, black-box AI recommendations without explanations produced better human judgment than those accompanied by rationales.",
        "execSummary":  "This research directly challenges the enterprise assumption that explainable AI is always better AI. When AI explains itself well, it may actually erode the human oversight that governance frameworks depend on. For customer-support and operations leaders deploying AI-assisted decision tools, the design implication is significant: surfacing AI rationales to front-line agents or managers may inadvertently reduce the quality of human review, not improve it. The key caveat to watch is whether this effect holds across domains beyond innovation screening; if it does, it reshapes how enterprises should design human-in-the-loop workflows.",
