        "summary":  "A Wharton study of over 1,400 enterprise leaders found that organizations with dedicated chief AI officers report stronger returns than those without. Companies using a single approved AI platform achieved 50% ROI compared to just 9% for those experimenting with multiple platforms simultaneously. The study emphasizes that dedicated responsibility and full authority matter more than titles alone.",
        "execSummary":  "The Wharton data delivers a clear strategic signal: platform consolidation and dedicated AI leadership accountability are the two highest-leverage structural moves for improving AI ROI. The 50% vs. 9% ROI gap between single-platform and multi-platform organizations should prompt immediate C-suite review of sprawling AI tool portfolios. The finding that authority and focus matter more than titles is also actionable: organizations with a CAIO in name only, buried under competing responsibilities, are leaving returns on the table. The watch item is self-selection bias: companies disciplined enough to consolidate platforms and create focused AI leadership roles may already have the management quality that drives ROI.",
