        "url":  "https://www.technologyreview.com/2026/10/02/1145639/dont-be-fooled-llms-dont-reason/",
        "date":  "2026-10-02",
        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Former DeepMind researcher Thore Graepel argues that large language models lack genuine reasoning capabilities despite impressive outputs, performing extended pattern completion through repeated token prediction rather than true deliberation. Unlike AlphaGo, which combined intuitive pattern recognition with explicit search through possible futures, today\u0027s AI systems do not maintain an inspectable epistemic state tracking hypotheses, evidence, and uncertainties. Graepel contends that trustworthy AI results in science and medicine will require systems with genuine reasoning capabilities fundamentally different from current LLMs.",
        "execSummary":  "A credentialed AI researcher who helped build AlphaGo is drawing a hard line between pattern completion and reasoning, and the distinction matters enormously for how enterprises deploy LLMs in high-stakes decisions. Customer-support and operations leaders who have deployed LLMs for case triage, contract review, or compliance tasks should treat this as a design constraint: LLMs will pattern-match confidently on novel situations where a reasoning system would flag uncertainty. The practical implication is to keep human judgment in the loop for any decision where novelty or edge cases are common, rather than assuming model confidence signals model correctness. This also informs vendor evaluation: claims of reasoning in AI product marketing deserve scrutiny against this technical baseline.",
        "vendorLean":  "Neutral",
        "imageUrl":  "https://wp.technologyreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/2llm-go.jpg",
        "isNew":  false
